    {
      "filename": "St Michael's of the Sea - geograph.org.uk - 1357198.jpg",
      "title": "File:St Michael's of the Sea - geograph.org.uk - 1357198.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/St_Michael%27s_of_the_Sea_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1357198.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Michael%27s_of_the_Sea_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1357198.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 480,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Barbara Carr",
      "description": "St Michael's of the Sea. This view shows the rounded end of the church 1357166.  The church at Bowmore on Islay is completely round - so that \"the de'il may not hide in the corners\": I don't know if the same principle applies here, or whether a rounded end is more able to cope with gales - there is a hand-rail all the way around the building from here to the main door, which hints at the strength of the winter winds.",
